<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Sebastian Bach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a>–<a href="/years/1750">1750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as J. S. Bach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Sebastian Bach was a German composer and organist, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>. He held posts at the small courts and towns of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>, where he wrote much of his instrumental music, before becoming cantor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1723. His works, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-concertos">Brandenburg Concertos</a> and the St Matthew Passion, are central to Western music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach.jpg" alt="Johann Sebastian Bach" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elias Gottlob Haussmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Sebastian_Bach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Johann Sebastian Bach</b> worked as cantor of St Thomas’s Church in Leipzig from 1723 until his death in 1750, and Dresden became a city of court music, painting and Baroque architecture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its demand that every Christian should know the scriptures encouraged schooling and literacy, and its hymns and chorales began a tradition of church music that led to the work of <b>Johann Sebastian Bach</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Johann Sebastian Bach</b> set it as a chorale, as a double canon in his Orgelbüchlein and as the chorale prelude BWV 729, written to accompany congregational singing in Arnstadt; another prelude long credited to him, BWV 751, has been attributed to Johann Michael Bach since the rediscovery of…</blockquote><a href="/songs/in-dulci-jubilo">In dulci jubilo</a></li><li><blockquote>Composers have set it since the Renaissance, among them Monteverdi in his Vespers of 1610, Vivaldi, <b>Johann Sebastian Bach</b> (1723, revised 1733), Bruckner, Rachmaninoff and Arvo Pärt.</blockquote><a href="/songs/magnificat">Magnificat</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Johann Sebastian Bach</b> used the hymn in his cantatas, among them BWV 79 and BWV 192, harmonised it for four voices and set it as a chorale prelude, BWV 657, among the Great Eighteen.</blockquote><a href="/songs/now-thank-we-all-our-god">Now Thank We All Our God</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Johann Sebastian Bach</b> arranged the melody and used five stanzas of the hymn in four settings in his St Matthew Passion.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-sacred-head-now-wounded">O Sacred Head, Now Wounded</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.98,10.32&amp;year=1727" aria-label="Eisenach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4785 396 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johann Sebastian Bach"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.98,10.32&amp;year=1727">What was here in 1727? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
